WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.180
Prepare two provider connections.

2
00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:09.500
Here we add a backup Codex connection, keep the official address, and
select the system proxy if needed.

3
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:17.162
Create a model route named demo-balanced, select the weighted strategy,
and set the maximum attempts to two.

4
00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:21.217
Add a model from each provider to the route.

5
00:00:21.237 --> 00:00:25.092
Set both tiers to zero, with weights of three and one.

6
00:00:25.112 --> 00:00:29.262
Requests are balanced by weight within the same tier and health state.

7
00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:34.918
For primary and backup operation, change the strategy to failover.

8
00:00:34.938 --> 00:00:39.055
Keep the primary at tier zero and set the backup to tier one.

9
00:00:39.075 --> 00:00:41.600
The lower tier is preferred.

10
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:49.600
Next, make the primary return a simulated HTTP 503 error to check
whether the backup can take over.

11
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:53.705
The client still requests the same route name.

12
00:00:53.725 --> 00:00:57.100
The reply shown here comes from a real request.

13
00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:01.205
Open the logs using the request ID.

14
00:01:01.225 --> 00:01:05.642
The primary returned 503 and the backup returned 200.

15
00:01:05.663 --> 00:01:09.413
One downstream request produced two upstream calls.

16
00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:15.350
After the demonstration, restore the primary provider’s normal address.
